<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="S-methyl-L-methionine cycle" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5441" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: CPD-397| (SMM) is synthesized from |FRAME: MET| by |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.12| (MMT), an enzyme unique to plants. SMM has been hypothesized to be an essential methyl donor or a transportable form of sulfur, although these roles remain to be tested. The SMM cycle has been hypothesized to rectify overshoots in the conversion of free methionine required for protein biosynthesis to |FRAME: S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE| (SAM) in the SAM cycle (|FRAME: PWY-5041|) by providing a way to convert the methionine moiety locked up in adenosylmethionine back to free methionine. Alternatively, the SMM cycle has been hypothesized to regulate the cellular SAM level, hence the methylation ratio. Characterization of an Arabidopsis MMT knock-out mutant, which has a significant higher level of SAM content and unaltered free methionine pool, indicates the SMM cycle contributes to regulation of cellular methylation ratio rather than preventing depletion of free methionine. The SMM cycle operates throughout the plant, and consumes half of the adenosylmethionine produced in Arabidopsis leaves.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE" />
    <Author name="zhang" />
  </Authors>
